  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nTemperatures will continue to trend above average in the coming\ndays, but today will be the last day of significantly low daytime\nRHs for the upcoming forecast period. As has been the case in\nrecent days, the combination of dry air and dry ground conditions/\ndrought will continue to elevate fire danger concerns, though a\nlack of significant wind will prevent the need for red flag\nproducts. While RHs will be higher beyond today and rain chances\nwill increase, meaning meteorological conditions will contribute \nless to fire concerns, antecedent ground conditions will still be \nproblematic in the coming days, particularly in areas that do not \nreceive significant rainfall. Continue to heed the advice of local\nand state fire officials and any burn bans.\n\nOtherwise, an upper shortwave will shift from the Southern Plains\ninto the Mid MS Valley over the weekend. Increasing mid level\nmoisture will result in increasing clouds by Friday, with virga\nlikely beginning to overspread the area by Friday afternoon as\nthis moisture encounters drier low levels. By early Saturday\nmorning, showers will be possible mainly around the ArkLaMiss/ \nDelta area. Across the remainder of the area, scattered showers \nmay mostly hold off until more into Saturday afternoon/evening, \ncontinuing into Sunday. Thunderstorms are also possible Saturday \ninto Sunday, but no severe weather is expected. Given the \nscattered nature of precip, many outdoor plans may still proceed \nwithout disruption this weekend, but it would be a good idea to \nkeep close watch on conditions and/or have a backup plan in case \nrain does occur at your location.\n\nA break in rain chances is still expected Monday as shortwave\nridging shifts across the area, then a deepening upper trough\nlooks to bring a more organized storm system to the area for the\nTuesday/Wednesday time frame. With this system, it appears more\nprobable showers and thunderstorms would be organized along a cold\nfront pushing eastward across the area. While instability with\nthis system is uncertain, stronger deep layer shear appears\nlikely, which could result in some strong storms. With this \nsystem still several days away, it is too early to have much \nconfidence in timing/threat details. /DL/",
